reversi on OS-9 -- what was changed===================================Elias Martenson's reversi, comp.sources.games volume 15, 1992.  GPL version2; the licence travels with it as ORIG/COPYING and SRC/reversi/COPYING.You play Othello against the computer or another person at the same keyboard,with six strengths from Apprentice to Very Hard, chosen from a menu.ORIG/ holds the posting's eleven files as they came.The port is entirely about the compiler, not the game.  Its README predictedmost of it: "The program should compile without problems on any ANSI-Ccompiler.  If You don't have an ANSI-C compiler at hand You might have tochange the struct initializations for the menu routines by hand."  Microware'scc is K&R, so that is exactly what happened.1.  FOUR MENUS WERE INITIALISED INSIDE FUNCTIONS.  K&R C initialises only    static and external aggregates, never an automatic one; c68 says `cannot    initialize'.  game_setup, select_player and level_menu were inside    main(), and game_menu inside do_game_menu().  All four are now static at    file scope, with a brace round each entry so the element count is    unambiguous.  The menus themselves are unchanged.2.  TWO PROTOTYPES.  `int menu( int x, int y, ... )' in menu.h, and    `char *getenv( char * )' in reversi.c.  Both are declarations rather than    definitions, so ansi2knr cannot help -- it rewrites a definition whose    name is at the left margin.  They are now `int menu();' and    `char *getenv();'.3.  TRUE AND FALSE were redefined.  reversi.h defines both and so does the    curses this builds against, identically; they are guarded with #ifndef.4.  THE `KNR' RECIPE FLAG IS NOT USED, AND MUST NOT BE.  It was tried first    and it BREAKS this tree: ansi2knr rewrites the ordinary K&R parameter    declarations that were already there, and c68 then reports `multiple    definition' on `main(argc, argv) int argc; char **argv;' and five more    like it.  Removing the flag fixed those six errors outright.  The note is    in tools/rebuild/README.md as well, because it is a rule about the flag    rather than about this program.WHAT IT NEEDS TO RUN.  TERM and TERMCAP, which SYS/login sets.  `reversi -r'resumes a saved game; the save file is written where you run it.